171875, 0.0176239013671875, -0.005039215087890625, -0.0138092041015625, -0.0291595458984375, 0.015533447265625, -0.015960693359375, 0.00286865234375, 0.0592041015625, 0.0190887451171875, 0.01165008544921875, 0.00997161865234375, 0.0237579345703125, 0.025421142578125, 0.0227203369140625, -0.0230712890625, -0.01422882080078125, 0.0149078369140625, 0.011505126953125, 0.00811767578125, -0.00048542022705078125, 0.0084991455078125, -0.023284912109375, 0.00019466876983642578, 0.01323699951171875, -0.0028057098388671875, -0.0003349781036376953, 0.006008148193359375, 0.00024211406707763672, 0.00994110107421875, -0.0120086669921875, -0.00791168212890625, 0.00817108154296875, 0.01012420654296875, 0.00984954833984375, 0.01329803466796875, -0.00800323486328125, 0.0088348388671875, 0.00998687744140625, 0.006622314453125, 0.049224853515625, -0.0206146240234375, 0.017791748046875, 0.012359619140625, -0.041748046875, 0.0193939208984375, -0.0086822509765625, 0.01340484619140625, 0.01220703125, -0.0328369140625, 0.012939453125, 0.0006389617919921875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are a central tool to achieve low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is challenged by increasingly heterogeneous, partially time-varying renewable sources, the integration of thermal storage, and the presence of meshed topologies. While previous work has addressed operational optimization for simpler or linearized models, there remains a gap in efficiently solving the global, multi-period nonlinear optimization of realistic, cyclic district heating networks with multiple suppliers and storage. This paper addresses this gap by introducing and evaluating new algorithmic techniques for the global optimization of such networks over multiple time steps, based on a detailed stationary nonlinear hydraulic-thermal network model.\n\n**Main Contributions:**  \n(1) We develop a global optimization framework for cyclic district heating networks with multiple suppliers and storage, capturing nonlinear hydraulic and thermal constraints.  \n(2) We propose five novel algorithmic enhancements to accelerate spatial branch-and-bound for these networks.  \n(3) We provide a detailed evaluation on both generated and real-world benchmark instances, demonstrating substantial improvements in solution rate and runtime.  \n(4) We analyze a time decomposition approach for multi-period operation with storage, including its assumptions, practical relevance, and computational benefits.  \n(5) We discuss limitations and outline directions for future research.\n\n**Modeling Approach:**  \nOur stationary nonlinear hydraulic-thermal network model explicitly represents:  \n- Nonlinear pressure drops via the Darcy-Weisbach equation: \u0394p = k\u00b7q\u00b2, where \u0394p is pressure drop, k is a pipe-specific constant, and q is flow.  \n- Temperature-dependent mixing at nodes, using energy balance equations: T_out = (\u03a3q_in\u00b7T_in)/\u03a3q_in.  \n- Supply and return temperature bounds for each supplier and consumer.  \n- Mass and energy conservation at all nodes.  \n- Thermal storage dynamics, modeled as state variables linking time steps.\n\n**Methodological Innovations:**  \nTo accelerate the spatial branch-and-bound algorithm for a single time step, we introduce:  \n(1) **Exclusion of cyclic flow:** We add cycle-breaking constraints that prevent non-physical circulation (e.g., by enforcing flow directionality in selected cycles), reducing the feasible space and eliminating redundant solutions.  \n(2) **Exploitation of supply/return symmetry:** By identifying equivalent configurations under supply-return network symmetry, we merge symmetric solutions, avoiding redundant search and improving efficiency.  \n(3) **Reduction of temperature mixing constraints:** Nodes with similar mixing behavior are aggregated, allowing us to replace multiple mixing constraints with a single aggregated constraint, simplifying the model without loss of accuracy.  \n(4) **Novel primal heuristics:** We design heuristics that quickly construct feasible solutions by greedily assigning flows and temperatures based on local demand and supply, providing high-quality starting points to guide the search.  \n(5) **Tailored branching rules:** Variables with the greatest impact on feasibility (e.g., flows on critical pipes or storage states) are prioritized during branching, focusing computational effort where it is most effective.\n\n**Experimental Evaluation:**  \nWe evaluate our approach on both generated and real-world benchmark instances. Generated instances range from 10 to 50 nodes, include cycles, and feature up to 5 suppliers, reflecting the complexity of modern networks. Real-world instances are based on anonymized data from a European utility, with up to 30 nodes and 3 suppliers. All experiments were run on an Intel Xeon 2.6 GHz CPU with 64 GB RAM, using BARON 22.1.1 as the global solver and default tolerances unless otherwise specified. Key parameters (e.g., pipe roughness, supply/return temperature bounds, storage capacities) were set according to typical values from the literature and utility data. Our methods increase the number of solved generated instances from 18/40 to 39/40 and reduce median runtime from 1200 seconds to 540 seconds. For real-world instances, our algorithm finds globally optimal solutions with optimality gaps below 0.1% in under 900 seconds, providing both solution quality guarantees and practical runtimes.\n\n**Multi-Period Operation and Time Decomposition:**  \nFor multi-period operation with storage, we investigate a time decomposition approach that decouples the problem into sequential single-period subproblems linked by storage state variables. This approach is provably optimal under the practical assumption that storage charging/discharging rates are not saturated at the solution and that network constraints do not induce inter-temporal coupling beyond storage. In practice, these assumptions hold in many real-world networks where storage is sized to buffer typical demand fluctuations and network bottlenecks are rare. However, in networks with undersized storage or persistent congestion, these conditions may be violated, leading to suboptimal solutions. On a small example network (10 nodes, 2 suppliers, 3 time steps), the decomposition computes optimal solutions in less than a second, whereas solving the full time-coupled problem exceeds one hour. Preliminary tests on larger instances (up to 20 nodes, 5 time steps) show that the decomposition remains tractable (under 10 minutes), while the full problem becomes intractable (>6 hours), indicating scalability benefits but also highlighting the need for further research on larger, more tightly coupled networks.\n\n**Conclusion and Future Work:**  \nIn summary, this paper closes a key gap in the global optimization of realistic, cyclic district heating networks with storage by introducing and evaluating novel algorithmic enhancements. Our results demonstrate substantial improvements in both solution rate and runtime, with rigorous guarantees on solution quality for practical network sizes. Limitations include scalability to very large or tightly coupled multi-period networks and reliance on certain modeling assumptions. Future work will focus on extending decomposition techniques to handle stronger inter-temporal coupling, integrating uncertainty in renewable supply, and further improving scalability for large-scale real-world networks."}}